/-!
# Transcendence degree one: F algebraic over K(t) for any transcendental t

Theorem statement: `AlgebraicCurve.isAlgebraic_adjoin_of_transcendental`
(`60dc6137-b64d-585a-9250-eab23c0a5809`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `4ad059e0-b94e-59d0-8eee-547b3ce24372`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K$ and $F$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra, and let $x \in F$ be an element such that $F$ is
algebraic over the intermediate field $K(x) =$ `IntermediateField.adjoin K {x}` generated by $x$
over $K$ inside $F$. Let $t \in F$ be transcendental over $K$, i.e. $t$ is not a root of any nonzero
polynomial over $K$ (`Transcendental K t`). The conclusion is that $F$ is algebraic over the
intermediate field $K(t) =$ `IntermediateField.adjoin K {t}`: every element of $F$ satisfies a
nonzero polynomial with coefficients in $K(t)$. No hypothesis of transcendence on $x$ is imposed;
the two hypotheses together force $x$ to be transcendental over $K$, since otherwise $F$ would be
algebraic over $K$ and no transcendental $t$ could exist. No assumption is made on the
characteristic of $K$, and $t$ is not assumed to lie in $K(x)$ or to be related to $x$ in any way.

This is the statement that in an extension of transcendence degree one every element transcendental
over the base generates a subfield over which the whole extension is algebraic; it is the standard
device for replacing a fixed coordinate $x$ of a function field of one variable by an arbitrary
uniformiser. It is used throughout the treatment of places and differentials of algebraic curves,
for instance in the lemmas on orders and differential coefficients at a place.

